{"version":2,"kind":"Article","sha256":"d7ef9f01e697332130b0042ec64740b224b579b0ae8ff491c87a31638060b608","slug":"stellar-calib-fuv","location":"/09_stellar_calib_fuv.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"Stellar Calibration of the UVIS FUV Channel","short_title":"Stellar Calibration FUV","authors":[{"id":"Donald E. Shemansky","name":"Donald E. Shemansky"}],"content_includes_title":false,"github":"https://github.com/cassini-uvis/users_guide","numbering":{"figure":{"enabled":true},"table":{"enabled":true},"code":{"enabled":true},"equation":{"enabled":true},"heading_1":{"enabled":true},"heading_2":{"enabled":true},"heading_3":{"enabled":true},"heading_4":{"enabled":true},"heading_5":{"enabled":true},"heading_6":{"enabled":true}},"copyright":"Cassini UVIS team","source_url":"https://github.com/cassini-uvis/users_guide/blob/jbook2/09_stellar_calib_fuv.md","edit_url":"https://github.com/cassini-uvis/users_guide/edit/jbook2/09_stellar_calib_fuv.md","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"09_stellar_calib_fuv.md","url":"/build/09_stellar_calib_fuv-e1283c79ab42b8ddec090e0d887928af.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Introduction","position":{"start":{"line":8,"column":1},"end":{"line":8,"column":1}},"key":"N1AV53l0ke"}],"identifier":"introduction","label":"Introduction","html_id":"introduction","implicit":true,"enumerator":"1","key":"KwDw9Xfil3"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The UVIS fuv channel has been calibrated on an absolute scale using observations of\nαVir. A model of the stellar spectrum is used in the analysis to accurately account for instrument\nproperties. The absolute scale is fixed by calibrating the αVir spectrum against the observations\nobtained using the Copernicus and IUE ( ","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"JC2gNM12kh"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cp-IUE","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"GPnCL4V0DW"}],"key":"gsTldCpJpK"},{"type":"text","value":" ) spectrographs as benchmark references. It has\nbeen found that the laboratory based calibration of UVIS FUV agrees with the absolute scale\nestablished by ","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"lXRpdVruQv"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cp-IUE","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"ZVgw2fcyi4"}],"key":"B8eBhPGmFn"},{"type":"text","value":" in the mid wavelength range 1300 - 1500 Å. The results of the analysis\nshows a variable sensitivity for UVIS fuv in the long wavelength region as a function of time in\nthe mission. The overall sense of the change at longer wavelengths is toward higher sensitivity as\ntime progresses. The magnitude of the change is an order of magnitude at 1900 Å. In the cases\nwhere the star calibrations cover all of the spatial pixels a flatfield is established. The flatfields\ncalculated here are preliminary and will require corrections in a few particular locations in the\nspectrum where narrow spectral features in the stellar spectrum are not accurately modeled. Only\na few of the available αVir stellar calibration sequences have been reduced with the\nmethodology applied here, but the full set of available observations can be reduced without a\nlarge expenditure of effort based on the present experience. The results obtained in this chapter\nindicate that the understanding of the physics of scattered solar radiation by aerosols and surface\nreflection will be significantly changed through the use of the spectral sensitivity curves\nprovided in this report. This investigation was undertaken after finding that theoretical aerosol\nscattering properties could not be reconciled with observed UVIS fuv spectra.\nThe calibration curves developed in this chapter differ from those described in Chapter 3\nbecause we use here a forward modeling approach. Our analysis and data reduction use forward\nmodeling for the purpose of accurately accounting for the effects of the instrument point spread\nfunction (psf). The calibration data submitted to the PDS are described in Chapter 3, and account\nfor temporal changes in sensitivity. Differences in the sensitivity curves presented in this chapter\nand those in the PDS are caused by psf effects, differences in flatfield, and in particular locations\nin the spectrum. The flatfields developed here are designed to operate on prefiltered data vectors.\nComparison of the impact of these different methodologies have not been investigated in detail,\nbut will be examined in the future as a means of determining limitation in accuracy of reduction\nprocesses. The data treatment described in this chapter is designed for detailed spectral modeling\nand application to the construction of image cubes from UVIS system scan observations that use\nthe entire slit in accumulating virtual images.\nFuture deeper exposures with multiple scans of αVir will be conducted once per year to\nprovide updated accurate flatfields. The current standard starcals are adequate for determining\nthe time dependent spectral sensitivity.","position":{"start":{"line":10,"column":1},"end":{"line":10,"column":1}},"key":"YfkBVuspQj"}],"key":"Dz8kZFHSe1"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stellar model and the absolute flux reference","position":{"start":{"line":45,"column":1},"end":{"line":45,"column":1}},"key":"ibLwM9JdTS"}],"identifier":"stellar-model-and-the-absolute-flux-reference","label":"Stellar model and the absolute flux reference","html_id":"stellar-model-and-the-absolute-flux-reference","implicit":true,"enumerator":"2","key":"El6ERzOYlr"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":61,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The αVir of model emission properties at the surface of the star have been obtained from\nthe detailed non-LTE (nLTE) blanketed calculations by R. Kurucz and J. Aufdenberg. The model\nparameters have been determined here by testing a range of blackbody temperatures underlying\nthe nLTE blanket in order to fit the ","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"WrlEvw1t4r"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cp-IUE","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"AEAliZPVBO"}],"key":"Zai0AFqM60"},{"type":"text","value":" observed spectra. The LISM extinction properties\nhave been roughly approximated in order to provide an accurate fit to the ","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"ytNb7JTDQN"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cp-IUE","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"BtScxbQIod"}],"key":"kgdkxkK2ly"},{"type":"text","value":" spectra > 1150\nÅ.\nThe LISM properties should be refined to provide an accurate model at shorter wavelengths.\nOur nominal stellar model at the observing instrument that conforms to the ","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"V9R5bDYYQb"},{"type":"emphasis","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Cp-IUE","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"LmtFEBb4eW"}],"key":"w6sCLTf8iS"},{"type":"text","value":" spectra falls\nwithin the uncertainties in visible-infrared measurements of surface temperature, stellar radius,\nand distance to the star. αVir is a binary and both components are included in the present model,\nalthough the smaller companion has only a small effect. The stellar parameters are given in Table ","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"XnxehrVdJ9"},{"type":"crossReference","kind":"table","identifier":"tab:alpha_vir","label":"tab:alpha_vir","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table ","key":"G1xUu7SnbH"},{"type":"text","value":"1","key":"IpkTBspasv"}],"template":"Table %s","enumerator":"1","resolved":true,"html_id":"tab-alpha-vir","key":"OOMtBbckiS"},{"type":"text","value":".\nThe Copernicus spectrum is obtained from Code et al.(1979) (see Herbison-Evans et al.,\n1971; Rogerson et a., 1973; Code et al., 1970; Meade & Code, 1980), and the IUE spectrum,\nswp50032rl, from M. Festou (private communication).","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"SBsiewCf2j"}],"key":"fmOOiaeD4f"},{"type":"container","kind":"table","children":[{"type":"caption","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"captionNumber","kind":"table","label":"\"tab:alpha_vir\"","identifier":"tab:alpha_vir","html_id":"tab-alpha-vir","enumerator":"1","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Table ","key":"IwPeptTU64"},{"type":"text","value":"1","key":"PSaV3CURMx"},{"type":"text","value":":","key":"VvSoFYdLUM"}],"template":"Table %s:","key":"s0d1UUpIZ4"},{"type":"text","value":"Physical Properties of αVir.","position":{"start":{"line":63,"column":1},"end":{"line":63,"column":1}},"key":"t3N3ZOLuig"}],"key":"E5eErJ08yt"}],"key":"DH3AwAMZJ3"},{"type":"table","position":{"start":{"line":67,"column":1},"end":{"line":70,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableRow","position":{"start":{"line":67,"column":1},"end":{"line":67,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"tableCell","header