er wavelengths the temporal trend is consistently toward higher\nsensitivity with advancing time. At 1900 Å the laboratory determined sensitivity is below the\nvalue in 2005 by a factor of ~8.","position":{"start":{"line":154,"column":1},"end":{"line":154,"column":1}},"key":"JVjsjaylNN"}],"key":"vWawGbm8vZ"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":163,"column":1},"end":{"line":165,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure 9.4: Sensitivity of the UVIS fuv channel determined from the laboratory calibration, compared to results\nfrom observations of αVir in 2001, 2003, and 2005. The sensitivity refers to a monochromatic source at a given\nwavelength.","position":{"start":{"line":163,"column":1},"end":{"line":163,"column":1}},"key":"tFP2lqdQiL"}],"key":"f1MZcTxNw9"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":167,"column":1},"end":{"line":168,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure 9. 5 gives the fundamental intrinsic sensitivity in units of counts per photon for the\nUVIS fuv corresponding to the Rayleigh brightness scale given in Figure 9. 4.","position":{"start":{"line":167,"column":1},"end":{"line":167,"column":1}},"key":"xGYDKqlZ5o"}],"key":"Bw8F1P8FYc"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":171,"column":1},"end":{"line":172,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Figure 9.5: Intrinsic monochromatic efficiency, ε (counts/photon), of the UVIS fuv channel determined from the\nlaboratory calibration, compared to results from observations of αVir in 2001, 2003, and 2005.","position":{"start":{"line":171,"column":1},"end":{"line":171,"column":1}},"key":"gn9FsFQWrB"}],"key":"fYkfMdb3UC"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":174,"column":1},"end":{"line":174,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Discussion","position":{"start":{"line":174,"column":1},"end":{"line":174,"column":1}},"key":"TFgcaTII88"}],"identifier":"discussion","label":"Discussion","html_id":"discussion","implicit":true,"enumerator":"4","key":"rjpMLrJsv9"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":176,"column":1},"end":{"line":186,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In general, the sensitivity curves should be used with the corresponding flatfield vectors\nobtained using the methodology applied here, because the sensitivity varies from row to row.\nUsers interested in this approach should contact the author directly for the latest flat field\ncorrections. In Section 9.3, it was pointed out that selected data values in fuv spectral pixels\n(pxw) are not removed from the analysis as they have been in standard reductions at LASP (See\nChapter 3). All pxw values are given equal weight in the current approach described in this\nchapter, which is designed specifically to ensure integrity for the reduction of system scans and\nemission spectral analysis. The flatfield vectors obtained with the present derivation will require\nadjustment at a few specific spectral locations where artifacts result from structure in the αVir\nspectrum. Future adjustments will be made using flatfields developed from LISM exposures. The\nLISM flatfields will be systematically compared to the stellar reductions to ensure integrity.","position":{"start":{"line":176,"column":1},"end":{"line":176,"column":1}},"key":"hwLoFLdTHT"}],"key":"xlPyzHJA0p"}],"key":"LIURYzo6fs"}],"key":"O0tUTefolY"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Reflectance Spectroscopy of Icy Moons and Saturn","short_title":"Icy Moons","url":"/icy-moons","group":"Cassini UVIS User Guide"},"next":{"title":"UVIS EUV/FUV Channel Saturn and Titan Occultation Reduction","short_title":"Saturn and Titan Occultation Reduction","url":"/sat-titan-occ","group":"Cassini UVIS User Guide"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}